import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Predicting RNA-seq Coverage from DNA Sequence as a Unifying Model of Gene Regulation";
const description = "Comprehensive technical summary and deep literature deconstruction of Borzoi (Linder et al., Calico Life Sciences, Nature Genetics 2025): Predicting strand-specific RNA-seq coverage from 524 kb DNA sequence at 32 bp resolution across transcription, alternative splicing, and polyadenylation.";
const readingTime = "22 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <!-- Back to Catalog -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Regulatory Genomics & Deep Learning</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Nature Genetics · Vol 57, April 2025, 949–961</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Johannes Linder*, Divyanshi Srivastava, Han Yuan, Vikram Agarwal, and David R. Kelley*</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> Calico Life Sciences LLC, South San Francisco, CA; Department of Biology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-024-02053-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1038/s41588-024-02053-6</a> · Corresponding Authors: Johannes Linder (<code>johannes.linder@calicolabs.com</code>), David R. Kelley (<code>drk@calicolabs.com</code>)</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Overview -->
    <p class="paper-lead">
      How can a single neural network predict the complex, multi-layered regulatory consequences of non-coding genetic variants across the entire human genome? <strong>Borzoi</strong> models strand-specific <strong>RNA-seq coverage profiles</strong> directly from <Katex tex="524,288\text{ bp}" /> (<Katex tex="524\text{ kb}" />) raw DNA sequences at <Katex tex="32\text{ bp}" /> resolution. By learning from RNA-seq alongside CAGE, DNase-seq, and ChIP-seq across human and mouse genomes (7,611 human and 2,058 mouse tracks), Borzoi unifies <strong>transcription initiation, alternative splicing, and polyadenylation</strong> into a single end-to-end framework that rivals or outperforms specialized single-task predictors.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. Executive Summary & Paradigm Shift</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rnaseq-challenges">2. The Core Modeling Challenges of RNA-seq</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">3. The Borzoi Neural Architecture & Tensor Flow</a></li>
        <li><a href="#variant-stats">4. Mathematical Variant Effect Scoring Statistics</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">5. Section-by-Section Results Breakdown (Figures 1–7)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#motif-syntax">6. Cis-Regulatory Syntax & Saliency Attribution</a></li>
        <li><a href="#formulations">7. Mathematical Formulations & Multi-Task Loss</a></li>
        <li><a href="#conclusions">8. Discussion, Limitations & Ecosystem Impact</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>1. Executive Summary & The Unification Paradigm Shift</h2>
      <p>
        Prior sequence-to-function models (e.g. <strong>Basenji</strong>, <strong>Enformer</strong>) focused almost exclusively on point-source chromatin assays—such as CAGE transcription start sites (TSS), DNase-seq/ATAC-seq open chromatin peaks, and ChIP-seq histone modifications. These assays produce localized, narrow read peaks that are straightforward to model with standard convolutional architectures.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, point-source models are fundamentally blind to downstream post-transcriptional processing: <strong>alternative splicing</strong>, <strong>exon inclusion/skipping</strong>, <strong>intron retention</strong>, and <strong>alternative polyadenylation (APA)</strong>. Conversely, specialized models (e.g. <strong>SpliceAI</strong> for splicing, <strong>APARENT2</strong> for polyadenylation) operate only on localized, isolated sequence windows and lack tissue-specific chromatin context.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The Borzoi Unifying Breakthrough
        </div>
        <p>
          By predicting <strong>continuous, strand-specific RNA-seq coverage tracks</strong> across hundreds of cell types and tissues, <strong>Borzoi</strong> naturally learns the complete lifespan of an RNA transcript: promoter activation, transcription elongation across exons, splice site recognition, and 3' cleavage/polyadenylation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Evaluation Dimension</th>
              <th>Point-Source Models (Enformer / Basenji)</th>
              <th>Specialized Splicing/APA Tools (SpliceAI / APARENT2)</th>
              <th>Unified Borzoi Architecture (Calico)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Primary Target Assays</strong></td>
              <td>CAGE, DNase-seq, ChIP-seq read peaks.</td>
              <td>Synthetic reporter constructs or binary splice junction donor/acceptor labels.</td>
              <td><strong>Strand-specific RNA-seq coverage</strong> + CAGE, DNase, ATAC, ChIP-seq.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Receptive Field Context</strong></td>
              <td>100 kb (Enformer) to 131 kb (Basenji2).</td>
              <td>10 kb (SpliceAI) / 200 bp (APARENT2) local sequence only.</td>
              <td><strong>524,288 bp (524 kb)</strong> broad chromosome window.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Spatial Resolution</strong></td>
              <td>128 bp bins (too coarse for splice boundaries).</td>
              <td>1 bp single-base resolution (local motifs only).</td>
              <td><strong>32 bp bins</strong> spanning 6,144 central output bins.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Regulatory Scope</strong></td>
              <td>Promoter and distal enhancer activation only.</td>
              <td>Splicing only or 3' UTR cleavage only (blind to enhancers).</td>
              <td><strong>Transcription initiation, alternative splicing, and 3' polyadenylation simultaneously.</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Tissue Specificity</strong></td>
              <td>Cell-type specific chromatin profiles.</td>
              <td>Tissue-agnostic (single generic prediction for all cell types).</td>
              <td><strong>31 GTEx adult tissues</strong> + hundreds of ENCODE/mouse cell types.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="rnaseq-challenges">
      <h2>2. The Core Modeling Challenges of RNA-seq</h2>
      <p>
        Predicting raw RNA-seq coverage from DNA sequence was historically considered intractable in regulatory genomics due to four fundamental mathematical and experimental hurdles:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Broad Spatial Span Across Disjoint Exons:</strong> Unlike narrow 200 bp ChIP-seq transcription factor binding peaks, RNA-seq reads span tens to hundreds of kilobases across multiple disjoint exons separated by massive introns (often <Katex tex="> 50\text{ kb}" />).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Extreme Dynamic Range:</strong> Gene expression levels vary over <Katex tex="> 10^5" /> orders of magnitude across cell types and developmental states, from lowly expressed long non-coding RNAs to hyper-expressed housekeeping transcripts (e.g. *ACTB*, *GAPDH*).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sharp Intron-Exon Discontinuities:</strong> The neural network must output near-zero read density in introns while maintaining precise, high-coverage plateaus across sharp exonic boundaries (often spanning only 100–200 bp).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Experimental Background & 3' Biases:</strong> RNA-seq libraries contain non-uniform coverage profiles caused by reverse transcriptase processivity, sequence-dependent priming bias, GC-content bias, and 3' poly(A) enrichment.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Borzoi overcomes these hurdles through a hybrid multi-scale architecture: a deep convolutional residual stem, a 14-layer transformer trunk capturing long-range enhancer-promoter looping, and high-resolution transposed convolution upsampling with U-Net skip connections.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="architecture">
      <h2>3. The Borzoi Neural Architecture & Tensor Flow</h2>
      <p>
        Borzoi takes as input a one-hot encoded DNA sequence <Katex tex="\mathbf{X} \in \{0, 1\}^{524,288 \times 4}" /> (<Katex tex="524\text{ kb}" />) and outputs predicted coverage across <strong>7,611 functional genomics tracks in human</strong> and <strong>2,058 tracks in mouse</strong>. The complete feed-forward tensor path proceeds as follows:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="cpu" size={15} /> Layer-by-Layer Tensor Pipeline
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>1. Input Representation:</strong> One-hot encoded nucleotide matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{524,288 \times 4}" />.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>2. Convolutional Stem:</strong> 7 residual convolutional blocks with max-pooling (factor 16,384 total downsampling). Channels progressively expand: <Katex tex="256 \to 384 \to 512 \to 640 \to 768 \to 896 \to 1024" /> with kernel sizes of 15 and 5, extracting local motif syntax and nucleotide k-mers.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>3. Transformer Trunk:</strong> 14 Multi-Head Self-Attention (MHSA) transformer layers with 8 attention heads, key/query dimension 64, and relative positional embeddings (RoPE/ALiBi/FIRE). Self-attention propagates distal regulatory signals across the central <Katex tex="196.6\text{ kb}" /> receptive field.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>4. Transposed Convolution & U-Net Upsampling:</strong> Transposed convolution layers upsample the latent representation back to <Katex tex="32\text{ bp}" /> spatial resolution, concatenating high-resolution spatial feature maps from early convolutional layers via U-Net skip connections.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>5. Species-Specific Multi-Track Output Heads:</strong> Linear projection followed by softplus activation, generating predicted non-negative read counts <Katex tex="\hat{\mathbf{Y}} \in \mathbb{R}^{6,144 \times 7,611}" /> across <Katex tex="B = 6,144" /> bins of size <Katex tex="32\text{ bp}" /> covering the central <Katex tex="196.6\text{ kb}" /> region.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Architecture Stage</th>
              <th>Input Tensor Shape</th>
              <th>Operation / Kernel</th>
              <th>Output Tensor Shape</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1. DNA Input</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="524,288 \times 4" /></td>
              <td>One-hot sequence encoding</td>
              <td><Katex tex="524,288 \times 4" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>2. Residual Conv Stem</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="524,288 \times 4" /></td>
              <td>7x Conv Blocks (kernel=15/5, pooling=2x)</td>
              <td><Katex tex="32 \times 1024" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>3. Transformer Trunk</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="32 \times 1024" /></td>
              <td>14x MHSA Layers (8 heads, rel-pos)</td>
              <td><Katex tex="32 \times 1024" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>4. Transposed Conv (U-Net)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="32 \times 1024" /></td>
              <td>Transposed conv + Skip concatenation</td>
              <td><Katex tex="6,144 \times 512" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>5. Final Track Head</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="6,144 \times 512" /></td>
              <td>Linear projection + Softplus activation</td>
              <td><Katex tex="6,144 \times 7,611" /></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="variant-stats">
      <h2>4. Mathematical Variant Effect Scoring Statistics</h2>
      <p>
        To evaluate a genetic variant, Borzoi performs forward passes on both the reference sequence (<Katex tex="\mathbf{X}_{\text{ref}}" />) and alternative sequence (<Katex tex="\mathbf{X}_{\text{alt}}" />), generating predicted coverage profiles <Katex tex="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\text{ref}}" /> and <Katex tex="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{\text{alt}}" />. Borzoi then derives specialized statistics to isolate specific regulatory mechanisms:
      </p>

      <!-- Stat 1 -->
      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">1. Gene Expression Effect Size (ΔGene / SUM)</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\Delta \text{Gene} = \log\left( \sum_{b \in \text{exons}} \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b} + 1 \right) - \log\left( \sum_{b \in \text{exons}} \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b} + 1 \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          Sums predicted strand-specific RNA-seq coverage across all annotated exonic bins <Katex tex="b \in \text{exons}" /> of the target transcript, isolating overall transcriptional abundance changes.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stat 2 -->
      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">2. Alternative Promoter Usage (ΔTSS)</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\Delta \text{TSS} = \log\left( \frac{\max_{b \in \text{TSS}_{\text{3'}}} \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b}}{\max_{b \in \text{TSS}_{\text{5'}}} \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b}} \right) - \log\left( \frac{\max_{b \in \text{TSS}_{\text{3'}}} \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b}}{\max_{b \in \text{TSS}_{\text{5'}}} \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b}} \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          Quantifies the change in coverage ratio between the 9 bins downstream of the 3'-most vs. 5'-most annotated TSS in GENCODE / FANTOM5.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stat 3 -->
      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">3. Alternative Polyadenylation Effect Size (ΔAPA)</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\Delta \text{APA} = \log\left( \frac{\bar{y}_{\text{distal, alt}}}{\bar{y}_{\text{proximal, alt}}} \right) - \log\left( \frac{\bar{y}_{\text{distal, ref}}}{\bar{y}_{\text{proximal, ref}}} \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\bar{y}_{\text{distal}}" /> = Average coverage across the 4 bins immediately upstream of the distal-most poly(A) site in PolyASite 2.0 &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\bar{y}_{\text{proximal}}" /> = Average coverage across the 4 bins upstream of the proximal-most poly(A) site.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stat 4 -->
      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">4. Splicing Perturbation Statistic (ΔSplicing)</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="S_{\text{splicing}} = \sum_{b \in \text{gene}} \left| \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b} - \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b} \right| - \left| \sum_{b \in \text{gene}} \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b} - \sum_{b \in \text{gene}} \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b} \right|" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          Subtracts total net expression change from the sum of absolute bin-level changes, isolating isoform redistribution, exon skipping, and intron retention independent of overall transcriptional upregulation/downregulation.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stat 5 -->
      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">5. Bin-Level L2 Difference Score (Sniff Fine-Mapping Metric)</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="L_2(t) = \sqrt{\sum_{b=1}^{B} \left( \log(1 + \hat{y}_{\text{alt}, b, t}) - \log(1 + \hat{y}_{\text{ref}, b, t}) \right)^2}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          Computes the Euclidean norm of log-fold coverage changes across all <Katex tex="B = 6,144" /> bins for track <Katex tex="t" />, serving as the foundational variant feature set for the <strong>Sniff</strong> fine-mapping framework.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="results">
      <h2>5. Section-by-Section Results Breakdown (Figures 1–7)</h2>

      <!-- Figure 1 -->
      <div class="paper-fig-card">
        <div class="paper-fig-header">
          <span class="paper-fig-badge">Figure 1</span>
          <span class="paper-fig-title">Borzoi Architecture & Genome-Wide Prediction Accuracy</span>
        </div>
        <div class="paper-fig-body">
          <p>
            Evaluating Borzoi on held-out test chromosomes across thousands of functional genomics tracks in human and mouse:
          </p>
          <ul class="paper-fig-takeaways">
            <li><strong>High Bin-Level Correlation:</strong> Achieved mean Pearson <Katex tex="R = 0.81" /> on CAGE, <Katex tex="R = 0.74" /> on DNase/ATAC, <Katex tex="R = 0.69" /> on ChIP-seq, and <Katex tex="R = 0.72" /> on RNA-seq coverage across held-out chromosomes.</li>
            <li><strong>Sharp Exon Boundaries:</strong> Successfully learned sharp splice junctions and zero-coverage intronic plateaus directly from sequence, without receiving hard-coded gene annotation boundaries during inference.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Figure 2 -->
      <div class="paper-fig-card">
        <div class="paper-fig-header">
          <span class="paper-fig-badge">Figure 2</span>
          <span class="paper-fig-title">Predicting Tissue-Specific Patterns of RNA-seq Coverage</span>
        </div>
        <div class="paper-fig-body">
          <p>
            Cross-tissue performance across 31 GTEx adult human tissues:
          </p>
          <ul class="paper-fig-takeaways">
            <li><strong>Lineage Clustering:</strong> Unsupervised PCA on predicted tissue tracks cleanly separated brain, liver, skeletal muscle, heart, and whole blood tissues.</li>
            <li><strong>Tissue-Specific Alternative Promoters:</strong> Accurately predicted tissue-specific alternative transcription start sites (e.g. liver-specific promoter usage in *LDLR* and *APOB* vs. brain-specific usage in *SYN1*).</li>
            <li><strong>Alternative Polyadenylation:</strong> Successfully predicted tissue-specific shifts toward proximal or distal poly(A) sites across tissues (e.g. *C1QBP*, *UQCRB*).</li>
