    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="motif-syntax">
      <h2>6. Cis-Regulatory Syntax & Saliency Attribution</h2>
      <p>
        To interpret how Borzoi computes its predictions, the authors use gradient-based saliency attribution and in silico mutagenesis (ISM):
      </p>

      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">Gradient Saliency Attribution</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="S_i(t) = \frac{\partial \hat{Y}_t}{\partial X_i} \cdot X_i" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="S_i(t)" /> = Importance score of nucleotide <Katex tex="i" /> with respect to target output track <Katex tex="t" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="X_i" /> = One-hot vector of the reference nucleotide.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Applying TF-MoDISco to these saliency matrices clustered thousands of motifs into non-redundant transcription factor families, revealing both known motifs and novel tissue-specific regulatory grammars.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="formulations">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulations & Multi-Task Loss</h2>
      <p>
        Borzoi is trained with a multi-task Poisson log-likelihood loss with spatial multinomial cross-entropy over spatial bins:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-equation-card">
        <div class="paper-equation-header">Multi-Task Poisson Log-Loss Formulation</div>
        <div class="paper-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{Poisson}} = \sum_{b=1}^{B} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \left( \hat{y}_{b, t} - y_{b, t} \log \hat{y}_{b, t} \right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="paper-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="y_{b, t}" /> = Normalized sequencing coverage count in bin <Katex tex="b" /> for track <Katex tex="t" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\hat{y}_{b, t}" /> = Model prediction with softplus activation &emsp;|&emsp;
